From f3a2c4e179f3c9bbd1cd4f2ab0975cc793c2edd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 390/488] soh: name the 23 address-named symbols defined in
 z_camera_data.inc

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 23 are defined in soh/src/code/z_camera_data.inc; updating their references touches 3 files in total.

  * D_8015BD7C -> sCameraPlayState
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the PlayState the camera code reads controller
    input through

  * D_8015BD80 -> sDbCamera
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the debug camera state Camera_Update hands out

  * D_8011D3AC -> sTargetCategory
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the actor category of the camera's current
    target, published for Camera_GetTargetCategory

  * D_8011D3B0 -> sAttentionYawOffsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the yaw offsets the attention camera tries
    when it needs a clear line to the target

  * D_8011D3CC -> sAttentionPitchOffsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the pitch offsets tried alongside them

  * D_8011D3EC -> sUnusedCameraFlag
    deduced, confidence GUESS: an s32 beside sUpdateCameraDirection that
    nothing in the tree reads or writes

  * D_8011D3F0 -> sCameraSkipFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: set to 3 on a camera reset and counted down so
    the main camera skips that many updates

  * D_8011D658 -> sCsSpiralOffsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the spherical offsets the demo 3 spiral
    cutscene interpolates the eye and at through

  * D_8011D678 -> sCsSpiralPoints
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the positions that same cutscene interpolates
    between

  * D_8011D6AC -> sAttentionPlayerFarKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the attention keyframes used when the target
    is the player and the eye is more than 30 units away

  * D_8011D724 -> sAttentionPlayerNearKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the same for a player target with the eye
    close in

  * D_8011D79C -> sAttentionDefaultKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the attention keyframes used for a target that
    is neither close nor a door

  * D_8011D83C -> sAttentionCloseKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the keyframes used when both the target and
    the player are close to the camera

  * D_8011D88C -> sAttentionCloseAltKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the alternative close-range keyframe set

  * D_8011D8DC -> sAttentionSwingKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the keyframes that swing around the target,
    scaled by its distance

  * D_8011D954 -> sAttentionDoorKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the keyframes used when the target is a door

  * D_8011D9F4 -> sAttentionFarKeyFrames
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the keyframes used for a distant target,
    scaled by the player-to-target distance

  * D_8011DA6C -> sSpec7EyeHighPoints
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the fixed eye positions the spec 7 camera uses
    above its height threshold

  * D_8011DA9C -> sSpec7EyeLowPoints
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the fixed eye positions it uses below that
    threshold

  * D_8011DACC -> sSpec7HighY
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the upper height threshold per spec 7 index

  * D_8011DADC -> sSpec7LowY
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the lower height threshold per spec 7 index

  * D_8011DAEC -> sSpec7Rolls
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the roll each spec 7 index reaches between
    those two heights

  * D_8011DAFC -> sDbgCameraSettings
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the camera settings the debug mode cycles
    through

